    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4d127d9b-65df-5c42-a6fb-2d3c46b02ba5",
      ":question/problem": "Given $y'(t)=-2+t^{6}$, find $y(t)$, the antiderivative of $y'(t)$.\n\n$y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $y'(t)=-2+t^{6}$.\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(-2+t^{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t &= \\int \\left(-2+t^{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= -2\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\int t^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= -2\\frac{t^{0+1}}{(0+1)}+\\frac{t^{6+1}}{(6+1)}+C \\\\ &= -2t+\\frac{t^{7}}{7}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAny additive constant in an antiderivative can be absorbed into the displayed arbitrary constant $C$.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "c9a052e2-c7cb-524d-9f9f-3eddf5be2458",
