      ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $y'(t)=-t^{5}-5$.\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\left(-t^{5}-5\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t &= \\int \\left(-t^{5}-5\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= -\\int t^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-5\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= -\\frac{t^{5+1}}{(5+1)}-5\\frac{t^{0+1}}{(0+1)}+C \\\\ &= -\\frac{t^{6}}{6}-5t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAny additive constant in an antiderivative can be absorbed into the displayed arbitrary constant $C$.",
